      "decision": "REJECT",
      "reason": "The end-to-end Dutch CDCR system and error analysis are relevant to F1, but the abstract does not identify a distinctive identity-evidence result beyond positional and graph modeling. Language coverage and generic chain-error analysis alone do not close the present gaps, and stronger papers already cover evidence composition and robustness. Identifier is UNCHECKED."
    },
    {
      "paper_id": "1906.01753",
      "decision": "ADMIT",
      "reason": "The paper directly analyzes cross-document event and entity coreference and reports that mention span, surrounding context, and predicate-argument relations each contribute to event-coreference decisions. That explicit component analysis is high-value evidence for determining which combinations can support same-event identity and is more informative for the project than another architecture-only CDCR result.",
      "stream_id": "F1",
      "role": "DIRECT",
      "confidence": 0.97
    },
    {
      "paper_id": "doi-10-1109-access-2021-3078096",
      "decision": "PENDING",
      "reason": "The abstract clearly concerns one-pass short-text stream clustering with evolving cluster representations, episodic memory, and replay, but it does not say whether an incoming item can create a genuinely new cluster, whether forced attachment occurs, or whether merge/split/retirement decisions are supported. Admit to F4 only if the full method has explicit new-cluster creation or comparable novelty handling and evaluates assignment behavior under stream evolution."
    },
    {
      "paper_id": "doi-10-1007-978-3-030-33223-5-12",
      "decision": "ADMIT",
      "reason": "The paper directly studies event-to-case correlation when events from multiple enterprise systems lack explicit case identifiers, using process-model and temporal evidence to assign events to process instances. This is strong transfer evidence for concrete longitudinal identity in an enterprise setting and is substantially closer to work-matter continuity than generic news or entity-resolution analogues. It does not establish Outlook/Teams production accuracy, so its role remains transfer. Identifier is UNCHECKED.",
      "stream_id": "F7",
      "role": "TRANSFER",
      "confidence": 0.96
    },
    {
      "paper_id": "doi-10-1016-j-ipm-2025-104085",
      "decision": "REJECT",
      "reason": "The method combines generated cross-document coherence with rhetorical discourse structure for same-event clustering, but this overlaps substantially with the admitted 2023 discourse-structure paper and other coherence candidates. The abstract does not show a uniquely important evidence ablation or identity-semantic contribution that warrants another reading slot. Identifier is UNCHECKED."
    },
    {
      "paper_id": "1805.10985",
      "decision": "REJECT",
      "reason": "The paper is genuine cross-document event-coreference work, but the abstract emphasizes supervised representation learning, clustering-oriented regularization, and agglomerative clustering rather than evidence for deciding same versus different events, uncertainty, partial identity, or longitudinal correction. It therefore adds less to the open gaps than the admitted F1 candidates."
    }
  ],
  "notes": "This gate intentionally does not admit generic CDCR architectures merely because they report clustering performance. The admitted F1 set was selected for evidence analysis, robustness against lexical overlap, explicit same-versus-different reasoning, or structural evidence. No candidate abstract provides direct production-domain evidence for longitudinal work-matter identity across Outlook email and Microsoft Teams, so F2/F10 and gap-4 remain open. None addresses calibration or selective prediction under distribution shift/concept drift closely enough to admit against F9, so gap-7 also remains open. The process-case paper is enterprise-adjacent transfer evidence only and must not be treated as validating msgloom production accuracy. The engineering questions about msgloom's false-merge/false-split costs, CONTINUE/NEW/UNCERTAIN policy, stable identity state, correction model, and bounded retrieval remain project-level experimental/design work rather than resolved literature claims."
